WHAT SHIPPED (env-gated OPT-IN, default byte-identical)
  `JXL_TREE_SELF_REPAIR=1` turns on a content-agnostic self-repair in the
  multi-group global modular tree learner (`section.rs
  write_global_modular_section_with_tree_dc_quant_knobs`). For a candidate that
  may be stride-aliased (large gather stride + non-trivial tree — the e5/e6
  8/16-bit tree-lift path), it:
    1. learns the tree from the DEFAULT fixed-stride sample (tree_a),
    2. cheaply pre-filters on the clustered/ideal cost RATIO (see below),
    3. if flagged, learns a SECOND tree from a DE-ALIASED randomized re-gather
       (tree_r, via the plumbed `TreeSamples::randomize_gather` +
       `next_subsample_gap`), and
    4. keeps whichever codes the ACTUAL residuals cheaper (real clustered ANS
       cost, 2 % moat).
  Decode is lossless-correct for either tree; only bytes differ. Because it
  keeps the cheaper tree, it can NEVER regress bytes — on non-aliased content
  the two costs sit within the moat so the fixed-stride tree is kept ⇒
  byte-identical (hash-locks 53/53, byte-lock, drift, full `it` 446/0 all green
  with the env unset).

THE TWO KEY FINDINGS (both refute earlier hypotheses)

1. NODE COUNT DOES NOT DISCRIMINATE ALIASING. The approved design ("keep the
   smaller tree") is WRONG: the aliased tree_a and the de-aliased tree_r are
   nearly the SAME SIZE (5336 e5: 641 vs 613 nodes) yet code 288 KB vs 211 KB
   (27 %). Photos over-split just as much (1508–7859 nodes) without aliasing.
   The over-split is a red herring — the aliased tree makes BAD SPLITS at
   similar size, it does not make MORE splits.

2. IDEAL PER-CONTEXT ENTROPY DOES NOT DISCRIMINATE EITHER. `estimate_token_cost`
   (the multi-seed picker's estimator) sees only a 1.7 % gap on 5336 (1148 k vs
   1128 k bits) because it models each context with its OWN ideal histogram. The
   REAL encoder CLUSTERS contexts to ≤96 histograms; the aliased tree's many
   thin contexts collapse badly under that cap. Only a REAL clustered ANS cost
   (`build_entropy_code_ans_with_options(.., enhanced_clustering=true, ..)` +
   write) reproduces the 27 % gap: 5336 tree_a=2290969 bits (real file 288293),
   tree_r=1674095 bits (real file 211098). THIS is the selection metric.

THE CHEAP ALIASING PRE-FILTER (bounds wall-time cost)
  The aliasing signature is a high clustered/ideal cost RATIO (a tree that loses
  a lot to the histogram-clustering cap). Measured tree_a ratios:
    5336 (SWITCH) 1.996   5334 (SWITCH) 1.302
    5335 (KEEP)   1.019   5340 (KEEP)   1.166
    photos (KEEP) 0.998–1.002   (clustering is ~free — contexts already similar)
  Gate at ratio > 1.05 (TREE_SELF_REPAIR_ALIASING_RATIO): non-aliased content
  skips the second tree-learn entirely. Set low so a borderline-aliased doc is
  never skipped — a false positive only wastes the second pass (real cost still
  keeps the fixed tree), a false negative would miss a fix.

MEASURED (e5, JXL_TREE_SELF_REPAIR=1 vs default; full table in the .tsv)
  STRICT byte improvement, ZERO regressions across 24 images (6 doc / 13 photo /
  3 shot / 2 clip):
    5336 doc  +36.9% → +0.3%  (SWITCH, roundtrip pixel-exact) — the headline fix
    5334 doc  -45.9% → -47.1% (SWITCH, extra 1.2pp)
    8100 shot +3.6%  → -0.4%  (SWITCH, now beats cjxl)
    9000 clip +16.8% → +10.3% (SWITCH, 6.5pp)
    ALL 13 photos: byte-IDENTICAL (KEEP) — no photo regression, unlike the
      unconditional JXL_TREE_SAMPLE_RANDOM (+0.7pp photo mean, 5335 +4.9pp).
    5335 doc stays +7.2% (KEEP) — random regressed it to +12.1%; cost-based keeps
      the fixed tree. THIS is why cost-based selection beats unconditional random:
      it is the max of both sampling schemes, never the worse of them.

WALL-TIME COST (the default-flip blocker)
  should_try fires broadly at e5-e6 (tree-lift stride ≥ 8, tree ≥ 256 nodes —
  photos included). With the ratio pre-filter:
    docs that SWITCH: ~1.75× (2× tree-learn + 2× collect + 2× clustered cost)
    photos that SKIP: ~1.3× (still pay ONE clustered-cost build for the ratio —
      real_ans_cost's write_tokens_ans over ~25M tokens is the cost)
  This is why it ships OPT-IN, not default-on: +30 % e5 lossless wall on ALL
  content (for the ratio's clustered build) is an rdtime regression that a
  default flip must not pay silently.

DEFAULT-FLIP PATH (follow-on, to win rd AND rdtime)
  The photo SKIP-path cost is ONE redundant clustered build: for a photo that
  KEEPs tree_a, the downstream real encode
  (section.rs Step 4 `build_entropy_code_ans_with_options`) rebuilds the SAME
  code the ratio gate already built (same tree_a, same tokens, LZ77 off at e5).
  Caching the loop's clustered_a code and reusing it downstream when tree_a wins
  makes the SKIP path free ⇒ default-on costs ~0 on non-aliased content. That
  reuse (thread an Option<pre-built code> through the `best` tuple) is the
  clean enabler for default-on; it is a real but localized refactor, deferred to
  keep this chunk shippable. Alternatively compute the ratio's clustered cost
  from clustered-histogram population (skip write_tokens_ans) — blocked today
  because OwnedAnsEntropyCode stores normalized ANS tables, not raw counts.

RELATION TO d0a304aa (JXL_TREE_SAMPLE_RANDOM)
  d0a304aa shipped the unconditional-randomize primitive (opt-in, forces
  randomized gather everywhere). This builds on it: same randomized gather, but
  learn-both + keep-cheaper-by-real-cost, so it captures the doc win WITHOUT the
  broad photo/structured-doc regression that made unconditional-random non-clean.
  Both stay opt-in; SELF_REPAIR is the smart superset.

LEDGER: HYPOTHESIS_LEDGER #24 (updated). e7 note: the self-repair fires only at
e5-e6 (tree-lift stride ≥ 8); e7's base path is stride-2 (frac 0.5, no tree-lift)
so should_try does not fire — fixing e7 aliasing additionally needs the secondary
tree_sample_fraction ×0.1 fix (see lossless_stride_alias_2026-07-15.meta §SECONDARY).

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
UPDATE — DEFAULT-FLIPPED (task #14, 2026-07-15, "perf-opt then flip")
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
The self-repair is NO LONGER opt-in. It is now DEFAULT-ON for the lossless
path and OFF for lossy. Two commits took it there:

  1. PERF-OPT (67d1dc91) — the default-on enabler. `real_ans_cost` builds the
     winning tree's ≤96-histogram-clustered ANS code; on the e5/e6 tree-lift
     path (LZ77 OFF) that build is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the downstream Step-4
     `build_entropy_code_ans_with_options` (same tokens, same num_contexts,
     lz77=None). Cache the winner's code and reuse it in Step-4 ⇒ a photo that
     KEEPs tree_a pays ZERO redundant clustered build. Byte-exact (5336=211098,
     photos identical); photo wall 1.3×→1.12×.

  2. THE FLIP — a plain `EffortProfile::tree_self_repair` field: `true` in
     `lossless_reference`, `false` in `lossy_reference`. `should_try` now takes
     the profile flag; `JXL_TREE_SELF_REPAIR=0/1` is a runtime OVERRIDE
     (=0 forces off for A/B, =1 forces on).

WHY NOT A gate_registry PER-STRATEGY GATE (the approved "off for Libjxl" shape)
  Investigated and REFUTED as inapplicable: the modular/lossless path reads NO
  `strategy` / `ResolvedImprovements` / `EncoderStrategy` (verified by grep); the
  lossless `EffortProfile` comes purely from `lossless_reference(effort)` with no
  strategy resolve (the resolve fn is documented "a no-op for lossless"); and the
  Libjxl byte-lock is `LossyConfig`-only. **Lossless output is STRATEGY-INVARIANT**
  — `EncoderStrategy::Libjxl` produces no distinct lossless bitstream (we already
  diverge massively from cjxl lossless — we BEAT it; there is no libjxl-lossless
  parity target). So "off for Libjxl" is vacuous for a lossless-only feature, and
  a gate-registry gate (which routes through the lossy ResolvedImprovements the
  lossless path ignores) would be non-functional. The correct realization of
  "flip default-on" is a plain profile field — which still wins rd AND rdtime.
  No drift-count bump (EXPECTED_DIVERGENCE_GATE_COUNT stays 32).

VALIDATED (target/release/cjxl-rs, DEFAULT features, e5 --lossless, NO env):
